          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry to hear about your diagnosis. Nothing in your message suggests an emergency. This is general information, not a judgement about your own case.\n\n**How blood pressure is classified.** The guidance in *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets* sorts adult office readings into four groups:\n- **Normal:** top number below 120 and bottom number below 80.\n- **Elevated:** top number 120 to 129, bottom number below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** top number at least 140, or bottom number at least 90.\n\nWhen the two numbers fall in different groups, the higher group applies. Your clinician can tell you which group your readings fall into.\n\n**Treatment.** The document *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line* says that treatment choice depends on other conditions you may have, such as diabetes or kidney disease. The passages I received stopped before they gave a specific target number, and they did not say which treatments are first-line for most people. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge, so I can't tell you more about treatment from these sources.\n\n**Questions you could bring to your clinician:**\n- Which stage applies to my readings, and what is my personal target?\n- Do my other health conditions or risk factors change the treatment choice?\n- Should I track my blood pressure at home, and how?\n- What lifestyle changes, if any, are recommended for me?\n\nThis is educational information only. It does not diagnose you, and it is not a substitute for advice from your own clinician about any medicine or treatment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
