          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about diet?",
          "answer": "The sources I found cover diet for high blood pressure only in part, and I can't give you a full diet plan from them.\n\n**What the guidance says.** The document *Diet for high blood pressure* describes two points:\n- **Weight:** Losing weight lowers blood pressure, roughly in proportion to the amount lost. The guidance says even a modest, sustained loss is worthwhile.\n- **Alcohol:** For people who drink well above a moderate amount, cutting back is described as one of the more reliable non-drug measures.\n\n**What the guidance says is not established.** The same document says there is no comparable evidence for single \"superfood\" items, vinegar, garlic supplements, or similar single-ingredient remedies. It also says habitual caffeine intake has little lasting effect on blood pressure for most people, although caffeine raises it briefly.\n\n**What I could not find.** The passages did not cover salt (sodium) limits, the overall eating pattern, or specific portion or food guidance. A heart-failure diet document mentions that a blood-pressure diet note covers salt, but the text I received does not include that note. I have not filled that gap from general knowledge.\n\n**Questions you could bring to your clinician or a dietitian:**\n- Is there a salt limit that suits me?\n- Would losing weight help my readings, and what amount would be a realistic goal?\n- How much alcohol is reasonable for me?\n- Do my other health conditions change what I should eat?\n\nThis is educational information only. It is not a substitute for advice from your own clinician or a registered dietitian.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
